    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Frequency-stabilized lasers are ubiquitous tools in laser spectroscopy and optical metrology. This thesis describes precision nonlinear spectroscopy of rubidium (Rb) at 780 nm that is achieved using the second harmonic of a diode laser at 1560 nm. The frequency of the laser is then actively stabilized to Rb resonances. It will ultimately serve as a frequency reference for optical time transfer and clock comparisons of frequency standards over a free-space optical link.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
